# argus-restore - restore the Argus core from an argus-backup archive (DESIGN section 14e,
# docs/backup-and-restore.md).
#
# Run as root on the core that should take over: a new core VM from the appliance image (first boot
# done, with any values: this replaces them), or a manual install of the same stack. It stops Argus,
# the updater and zabbix-server, puts back the configuration, keys and certificates, recreates the
# Zabbix database from the dump (with TimescaleDB's pre/post-restore steps) and the Argus database,
# then starts everything again. Probes reconnect on their own: the CA they trust comes back with it.
#
# A TimescaleDB dump restores only onto the extension version it was made on. When this machine is on
# another one (a core moved to the next TimescaleDB line since the backup, a new appliance image),
# the restore installs the backup's version first, older or newer, holds it and pins its line: this
# is the way back from a TimescaleDB line move (argus-tsdb-update).
#
# Usage:
#   argus-restore inspect ARCHIVE                show what an archive holds, check it, change nothing
#   argus-restore containers ARCHIVE             print the docker run commands the old core's Argus
#                                                containers ran with (a core installed by hand)
#   argus-restore network ARCHIVE [--yes] [--keep-hostname] [--static]
#                                                give this machine the old core's address and hostname
#   argus-restore restore ARCHIVE [--only files|zabbix|argus] [--yes] [--force] [--passphrase-file F]
#   argus-restore restore ARCHIVE --test        try a backup on a spare machine, next to the running core
#   argus-restore live                           end a test restore: this machine becomes the real core
# An encrypted archive (.tar.gpg) asks for its passphrase unless --passphrase-file is given.
#
# A TEST RESTORE. A restored core is a complete copy of the old one: the same channels, heartbeat URL,
# scheduled reports, syslog server and backup target. That is right when the old core is gone, and
# wrong for a test next to a core that still runs: the copy hears none of the probes (they talk to the
# real core), so it would report everything down through the real channels. `restore --test` leaves
# a mark in Argus's data folder before anything starts; while it is there Argus alerts nobody, mails no
# reports, sends no syslog, holds the heartbeat and hands argus-backup a plan without a target
# (argus/internal/testrestore), and `network` refuses to take the real core's address. `live` removes the
# mark: no restart, Argus looks every few seconds.
